    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Structured Output Parser\n\nA structured output parser tells the LLM to follow a specific response format.  \nIt narrows the model’s options so the output becomes predictable and consistent.  \nThis helps downstream nodes use the data without cleanup or guesswork.\n\n\n### JSON Example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Short description of what happened\",\n  \"category\": \"Finance\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.92\n}\n```\n\n### JSON Schema example\n\nJSON Schema is a more verbose way to describe your desired output structure to an LLM model. This higher verbosity is both good and bad:\nGood: More control\nBad: More to learn\n\n..it's basically way to use JSON to describe JSON :upside_down:\n\nHere's an example JSON schema that corresponds to the object above:\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"summary\": { \"type\": \"string\" },\n    \"category\": { \"type\": \"string\" },\n    \"confidence\": { \"type\": \"number\" }\n  },\n  \"required\": [\"summary\", \"category\"]\n}\n```",
        "height": 780,
        "width": 572,
        "color": 5
      },
